WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:09.337
Quincy and Becca sat in the dark classroom, fanning themselves.

2
00:00:10.277 --> 00:00:14.820
The other students had all gone home because of the blackout, but Becca's parents couldn't pick her up

3
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:20.503
early. Quincy had forgotten his house key, so he was stuck at school too, until his mom came.

4
00:00:21.364 --> 00:00:23.885
Mrs. DeWitt sat by the window, grading papers.

5
00:00:23.905 --> 00:00:27.868
"At least we got to skip the debates today," Quincy said.

6
00:00:28.944 --> 00:00:30.545
"Don't worry," Mrs.

7
00:00:30.585 --> 00:00:31.706
DeWitt said with a smile.

8
00:00:31.726 --> 00:00:34.587
"We'll hold the debates tomorrow."

9
00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:37.169
"I like to debate," Becca said.

10
00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:41.211
"But it's so hard to prepare when we don't know the topic ahead of time."

11
00:00:42.612 --> 00:00:43.613
Mrs. DeWitt nodded.

12
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:49.356
"It is, but I want you to think of strong arguments on the spot.

13
00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:56.140
Everyone has opinions—people should know how to support their opinions."

14
00:00:56.884 --> 00:01:01.467
"I definitely have an opinion about this blackout," Quincy groaned.

15
00:01:01.487 --> 00:01:05.129
"Being without power is boring and hot!"

16
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:14.454
"The utility company asked residents to conserve energy during the heat wave so we wouldn't have a blackout,"

17
00:01:14.794 --> 00:01:15.695
Mrs. DeWitt said.

18
00:01:15.715 --> 00:01:20.858
"But people must be using more power than the system can handle."

19
00:01:22.218 --> 00:01:24.800
Becca had been studying renewable energy in science.

20
00:01:26.344 --> 00:01:27.965
She remembered something that she had read.

21
00:01:27.985 --> 00:01:33.848
"People use less household electricity now than they did ten years ago.

22
00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:37.210
Appliances and technology are more energy efficient."

23
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.251
"Oh?" Mrs.

24
00:01:39.311 --> 00:01:39.932
DeWitt said.

25
00:01:39.952 --> 00:01:47.836
"Interesting "Well, I think we need more power plants to produce more energy," Quincy burst out.

26
00:01:47.856 --> 00:01:54.560
"The problem is, most power plants burn fossil fuels like coal, oil, or natural gas.

27
00:01:55.264 --> 00:01:56.685
And that's bad for the environment."

28
00:01:56.705 --> 00:01:59.067
"So what's the solution?"

29
00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:00.388
Mrs. DeWitt asked.

30
00:02:00.408 --> 00:02:03.050
"More people should use solar power!"

31
00:02:03.391 --> 00:02:04.151
Becca declared.

32
00:02:04.171 --> 00:02:07.154
"It's clean, renewable energy.

33
00:02:09.055 --> 00:02:14.520
In fact, enough solar energy hits Earth in one hour to provide all the power we need for

34
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:14.980
a year.

35
00:02:15.681 --> 00:02:19.644
With solar, families could cover all their household electricity needs."

36
00:02:20.845 --> 00:02:24.668
"Solar panels are expensive, and so is installing them," Quincy pointed out.

37
00:02:26.048 --> 00:02:28.169
"Plus, the panels aren't efficient.

38
00:02:28.689 --> 00:02:32.510
Only some of the sun's energy that hits them gets converted into electricity."

39
00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.671
"What's your idea, Quincy?"

40
00:02:36.031 --> 00:02:36.971
Mrs. DeWitt asked.

41
00:02:37.931 --> 00:02:39.092
Quincy thought for a moment.

42
00:02:40.132 --> 00:02:43.533
"Wind and water are renewable sources of energy, unlike fossil fuels.

43
00:02:44.173 --> 00:02:45.713
And they don't produce air pollution.

44
00:02:47.094 --> 00:02:49.194
We could build more wind turbines and dams."

45
00:02:50.595 --> 00:02:51.555
Becca shook her head.

46
00:02:51.575 --> 00:02:55.256
"Dams take up a lot of space and disrupt the environment.

47
00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.021
Even wind farms disturb the land's surface.

48
00:02:58.681 --> 00:03:02.263
These forms of power can only be built in places with water or wind."

49
00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:03.483
She grinned.

50
00:03:03.503 --> 00:03:06.505
"Solar panels don't disrupt the environment.

51
00:03:07.085 --> 00:03:11.307
And they can be installed anywhere the sun shines—like on peoples' roofs."

52
00:03:13.088 --> 00:03:17.149
"True, but people need a lot of panels to cover their entire usage.

53
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.832
They also need a battery system to store energy for when the sun isn't shining," Quincy said.

54
00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.562
"Dams and wind farms can produce larger amounts of electricity than solar."

55
00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:30.863
"I see your point," Becca said.

56
00:03:30.883 --> 00:03:35.625
"But if more people use solar, we could reduce our need for big power plants."

57
00:03:36.405 --> 00:03:40.567
"Wouldn't it be better to wait until costs go down and the technology is more efficient?"

58
00:03:40.827 --> 00:03:41.508
Quincy asked.

59
00:03:41.528 --> 00:03:46.130
"Nope! We need more solar power now," Becca said.

60
00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:51.452
"The more people use solar, the more costs will go down and the technology will improve."

61
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.261
Mrs. DeWitt began to clap.

62
00:03:54.281 --> 00:03:56.624
"Great job, you two."

63
00:03:57.504 --> 00:03:59.146
The kids looked at her, confused.

64
00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:02.469
"With what?"

65
00:04:02.729 --> 00:04:03.330
Becca asked.

66
00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:08.074
"With your arguments about renewable energy," the teacher said.

67
00:04:08.094 --> 00:04:12.578
"You each earned an A on tomorrow's debate assignment."

68
00:04:12.598 --> 00:04:15.981
"Yes!" Quincy sat back with a grin.

69
00:04:16.862 --> 00:04:18.043
But Becca leaned forward.

70
00:04:18.063 --> 00:04:19.164
"Who won?"

71
00:04:20.988 --> 00:04:22.590
"It was a tie," Mrs.

72
00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:23.230
DeWitt said.

73
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:28.935
"You both presented clear opinions and supported them with strong evidence.

74
00:04:29.475 --> 00:04:32.398
That's exactly what skilled debaters do."

75
00:04:34.059 --> 00:04:35.901
Just then the classroom phone buzzed.

76
00:04:39.564 --> 00:04:42.746
Mrs. DeWitt picked it up and spoke to the school secretary for a moment.

77
00:04:42.766 --> 00:04:48.411
"Quincy, your mom is waiting at the main office."

78
00:04:48.431 --> 00:04:54.793
"Great!" He grabbed his things and headed toward the door.
